  'Helical piers are steel shafts with welded bearing plates, rotated into the ground until they reach soil capable of carrying structural load. They develop capacity two ways -- bearing at the plates and friction along the shaft -- and installation torque is tracked as a working indicator of that capacity, though torque alone does not replace a real soil investigation. They are the right call when competent bearing soil exists at a reachable depth and access is limited; they are the wrong call when the actual problem is moisture, drainage, or a crack that never was structural to begin with.';
const description =
  'What helical piers actually are, how they carry load, and when New Mexico soil conditions call for them -- and when they don’t.';

