>
  <div class="tls-article" id="top">
    <section class="page-hero">
      <div class="page-hero-inner">
        <span class="hero-eyebrow">Methods · Helical Piers</span>
        <h1>Helical Piers —<br /><em>What They Actually Do</em></h1>
        <p class="page-hero-sub">Hearing the term on an estimate doesn't mean you need them. Here's what they are, how they carry load, and what New Mexico soil actually has to look like for them to be the right call.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div class="article-outer">
      <article class="article-body">
        <p class="article-lede">Helical piers are steel shafts with bearing plates, rotated into the ground until they reach soil capable of carrying structural load. They are not the same system as a push pier or a micropile, and they address one specific problem — bearing capacity — not every cause of foundation movement.</p>

        <div class="article-section" id="what-they-are">
          <h2>What a Helical Pier <em>Actually Is</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">A helical pier is a steel shaft with one or more helical bearing plates welded to it — installed by rotating it into the ground, like a very large screw, until it reaches soil capable of carrying structural load.</p>
          <p>The system has a lead section carrying the bearing plates (typically 8 to 14 inches in diameter), extension shafts to reach target depth, a coupling system joining those sections, and a foundation bracket connecting the finished pier to your structure. Unlike a push pier, which is driven using the structure's own weight as reaction force, or a micropile, which is drilled and grouted, a helical pier is turned into the ground under torque — no dead-load requirement, no drill rig.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="how-they-work">
          <h2>How They Actually <em>Carry Load</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">Capacity comes from two places at once, and they don't contribute equally.</p>
          <p>The helical plates create bearing surfaces at depth. Capacity increases with larger plate diameter, more plates, and placement in higher-capacity soil. Plate spacing matters: plates set too close together can overlap their stress zones and fail to develop independent bearing.</p>
          <p>Shaft friction adds to total capacity but is the less reliable contributor in most residential work — it matters more in cohesive clay than in loose granular soil. [TIM: how would you characterize the soils where you're typically installing these — mostly clay, mostly granular fill, mixed by region?]</p>
          <div class="callout-block">
            <span class="callout-label">Torque vs. capacity</span>
            <p>Installation torque is tracked and correlated to capacity, but the correlation has real limits. A hard layer can produce high torque with minimal long-term bearing capacity; a genuinely adequate bearing stratum can produce a lower torque reading than expected. Torque is a working indicator during installation, not a replacement for a soil investigation done before the pier ever goes in the ground.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="scope">
          <h2>What a Helical Pier <em>Does and Doesn't Address</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">Helical piers address load support — bearing capacity, competent soil at reachable depth. That's the whole scope.</p>
          <p>They do nothing for drainage problems, moisture infiltration, plumbing leaks, or seasonal clay expansion. Installing piers when moisture is the actual cause can stabilize elevation while the real problem keeps running underneath it.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="installation">
          <h2>What Installation <em>Actually Involves</em></h2>
          <p class="opening">Installation runs on hydraulic torque motors mounted on skid steers, mini excavators, or hand-held equipment depending on access — depth is reached when the target torque is hit, practical refusal occurs, or a predetermined depth from soil data is met, whichever comes first.</p>
          <p>[TIM: anything specific to how this plays out on New Mexico ground — caliche layers stopping a pier short of target depth, adobe or historic construction changing the approach, backyard/access realities in older Albuquerque or Santa Fe neighborhoods, anything else worth a homeowner knowing before the crew shows up?]</p>
          <div class="callout-block">
            <span class="callout-label">Scope exclusions</span>
            <p>Standard installation does <strong>not</strong> include drainage correction, interior finish repair, plumbing fixes, or cosmetic crack repair. If a quote includes piers, confirm in writing what's in scope and what isn't before work starts.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="article-section" id="misconceptions">
          <h2>Common <em>Misconceptions</em></h2>
          <ul class="key-points">
            <li><strong>"Helical piers are the only solution"</strong> — push piers, micropiles, and non-structural drainage work can all be equally or more appropriate, depending entirely on what the investigation finds.</li>
            <li><strong>"Torque guarantees capacity"</strong> — it's a useful working number during installation, not a substitute for knowing what's actually down there.</li>
            <li><strong>"Deeper is always better"</strong> — the goal is reaching competent soil, not maximum depth. Going deeper than the bearing stratum requires doesn't buy you anything.</li>
            <li><strong>"Every crack means I need piers"</strong> — most residential cracking is ordinary material behavior — shrinkage, thermal movement — not foundation distress. Telling the two apart is the entire point of an evaluation.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <a href="#top" class="back-to-top">↑ Back to top</a>

        <p class="closing-link"><a href="/foundation-repair-methods" class="body-link">← Back to Foundation Repair Methods</a> · <a href="/glossary#helical-pier" class="body-link">Glossary: Helical Pier →</a></p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

